To find the shortest distance from the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4x + 8y$ to the point $(5, -2)$, we can start by rewriting the equation of the circle in standard form.
First, we can complete the square for both $x$ and $y$:
\begin{align*}
x^2 + y^2 &= 4x + 8y \cdot 4x + 8y & \text{Complete the square} \\
x^2 - 4x + 4 + y^2 - 8y + 16 &= 20 & \text{Add 20 to both sides} \\
(x - 2)^2 + (y - 4)^2 &= 20 & \text{Simplify the left side}
\end{align*}
So the equation of the circle in standard form is $(x - 2)^2 + (y - 4)^2 = 20$.
The center of the circle is $(2, 4)$ and the radius is $\sqrt{20} = 2\sqrt{5}$.
To find the distance from the point $(5, -2)$ to the center of the circle, we can use the distance formula:
\begin{align*}
d &= \sqrt{(5 - 2)^2 + (-2 - 4)^2} & \text{Distance formula} \\
  &= \sqrt{3^2 + (-6)^2} & \text{Simplify} \\
  &= \sqrt{9 + 36} & \text{Evaluate the squares} \\
  &= \sqrt{45} & \text{Add} \\
  &= 3\sqrt{5} & \text{Simplify the square root}
\end{align*}
To find the shortest distance from the point to the circle, we subtract the radius of the circle from the distance to the center:
$$d_{\text{shortest}} = d - \text{radius} = 3\sqrt{5} - 2\sqrt{5} = \sqrt{5}$$
So the shortest distance from the circle to the point is $\sqrt{5}$ and $m = \boxed{5}$.

