&=\sqrt{\left(\frac{2b-2t}{\sqrt{2}}\right)^2}.
\end{align*} Substituting this value into our earlier expression for $t^2,$ we find \[t^2=\left(\frac{t}{\sqrt{2}}+\frac{b-t}{\sqrt{2}}\right)(\left(\frac{t}{\sqrt{2}}+\frac{b-t}{\sqrt{2}}\right)+2b+2c).\] Simplifying, \[t^2=\left(\frac{b}{\sqrt{2}}\right)\left(\frac{3b}{\sqrt{2}}+2c\right).\] Finally, note that the amount of horizontal and vertical distance that the point $(b,c)$ is from the origin is always $b_1^2+c_1^2=\left(\frac{3b}{\sqrt{2}}+2c\right)^2.$ So \[\boxed{t^2=b^4+c^4+6b^2c^2}.\]

